z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,k \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={(t - 2)}^{2},y={e}^{(t - 2)},$ and $z={e}^{(t - 2)}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(-{e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({(t - 2)}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t - 2)})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t - 2)})\\,k \\\\  & =(2(t - 2))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((-{e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,k)\\cdot ((2(t - 2))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,k) \\\\  & =-2(t - 2){e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t - 2)}+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t - 2)} \\\\  & =-2(t - 2){e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})+2{e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2(t - 2)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})-(t - 2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["9f7f1241-2ff1-5381-8dd4-2f497ed46b35"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "10ad4d6c-c271-5873-87a1-cccc6bed0a8